verdicts.corrected_statement:
The code-level facts are correct. When bs < MAP the write buffer leaks, so nothing in the FTL model throttles the host. Every user wordline holds one io-worker entry until its NAND completion. The queue holds at most 16383 entries per worker, and when it is full, internal ops and host commands are dropped silently after the FTL has been updated. Host insertion and the worker pass are both O(N). A LUN backlog of 393 ms or more makes chmodel_request print KERN_ERR and return zero transfer time.

The claimed consequences are partly wrong:
(a) Before GC, the O(N) dispatcher/worker cost is negative feedback that keeps pending internal ops bounded at about 1K. Host IOPS then equals the modelled NAND page capacity: measured 285.8K/s for MAP8K/bs4K and 142.8K/s for MAP16K/bs4K. It is not about 64K/s and not a CPU-cache artifact, and the queue does not overflow. In MAP8K/16K bs4K, fio completed on time with no WARNING.
(b) GC starts much earlier than simulated, at about 5.7 s. After that, GC NAND time (which has no queue entries) pushes the LUN backlog past the 393 ms horizon at about 16-17 s. This produces millions of KERN_ERR lines (6.8-8.2M per run, which overruns /dev/kmsg readers) and zero channel time, and IOPS keeps falling. Data from leaky cells after GC, or at least after the horizon, is unreliable. Whether the queue overflows in MAP64K/128K is not established.
(c) The validity check must not grep for 'IO queue is almost full'. WARN_ON_ONCE never prints that string. It prints "WARNING: ... __allocate_work_queue_entry" once per module load. Instead, check for:
- 'WARNING' or '__allocate_work_queue_entry';
- nvme timeout, abort or reset messages;
- fio finishing at about the runtime with all I/Os complete;
- the '[chmodel_request] Need to increase array size' and 'No free entry' counts.
Kmsg overruns can drop lines, so these checks must not rely on dmesg alone.

With WRITE_EARLY_COMPLETION=1 and no FUA, the modeled completion target (ret->nsecs_target) never includes NAND or GC time. In balanced cells (bs >= MAP), foreground GC reaches fio only in two ways:
- write-buffer-full stalls, where the SQE is not consumed and is retried with a fresh nsecs_start;
- dispatcher CPU spent in do_gc, which runs after the target is fixed.

In leaky cells (bs < MAP), the buffer check never stalls, but GC's NAND cost is not invisible. Each host wordline still schedules an internal op that stays in the worker work_queue until its NAND completion time. If NAND+GC demand exceeds NAND capacity (likely after GC starts for MAP >= 32K), the backlog grows without bound and shows up in fio in four ways:
- the dispatcher pays an O(N) sorted insert for every host command, and the workers do an O(N) scan on every pass;
- channel-model printk spam starts once LUN time is more than about 393 ms ahead;
- at worst, a worker's 16384-entry queue is exhausted. Host commands are then silently dropped (SQE consumed, no CQE), and fio hangs or times out.

The method section should say this, and leaky cells should be checked for dmesg errors and IO-queue warnings.

